{
  "_game": "Rush Hour (puzzle: slide cars to free the red car) -- the complete session of Rush-Hour's own program",
  "_note": "Setup: the `rushhour` extra (rushhour-gym>=0.7.0). On first use the package downloads the rushhour-env Go engine of the matching Rush-Hour release into ~/.cache/rushhour-gym/ (checksum-verified); set RUSHHOUR_ENV_BIN or the phase 'binary' field to use a binary of your own. The env is RushHourHuman-v0: the Rush-Hour program's own interface (button selection, its picture, its results columns in info), all in the package -- the adapter is a keymap plus the fields to log.",
  "_session_note": "Ports the participant flow of https://github.com/chrplr/Rush-Hour main.go as curriculum entries that session.py runs, one game phase per puzzle, each blocking (advancing_outcomes ['won']: a puzzle counts once solved, which is also the only way the engine ends one): this instruction screen, then the first 12 puzzles of the library in its order (easiest first, what Rush-Hour's -n presents), then the other 37 puzzles of the library (p13-p49) once each in a fixed shuffled order (random.Random(20260913).shuffle), 49 distinct puzzles in all; 'puzzle' names each. Each is preceded by a self-paced 'Puzzle i of N, press any key' message (not the first) and a blank 0.8 s message (the ITI), and followed by the block's own result screen -- 'Final score' and 'Puzzle solved', the rushhour adapter's outcome, for outcome_duration seconds (default 2; text on the plain background, not the solved board Rush-Hour holds). Each puzzle is its own block: one .npz and one manifest entry with onset/offset, and the puzzle number is the phase's place in this list -- the board itself carries no counter. The board is drawn like rushui (same layout, palette, white outline and arrows on the selected car). Logged variables carry the columns of Rush-Hour's results file (event, car, from_*/to_*, n_slides, solved, t_ms, trial_ms). Not reproduced: Rush-Hour stamps keypresses and flips on SDL's monotonic clock; here a key is timestamped when the loop processes it, i.e. within 1/fps -- hence fps 60 (a turn-based block only steps on keydown, so the rate costs nothing). max_duration is per puzzle and effectively unlimited; ESC is the experimenter's way out.",
  "_keys_note": "Env action space Discrete(12) = rushinput's meta-actions: 0..3 choose the car up/down/left/right (spatial, what Rush-Hour's gamepad stick does), 4/5 choose the previous/next car, 6/7 slide it back (left/up) / forward (right/down) along its own axis (the four-button box), 8..11 slide it left/right/up/down -- only along its own axis: a direction across it (LEFT on a vertical car) moves nothing and is logged as event 'wrong_axis'. The defaults below are rushinput.DefaultMap's keyboard (rushhour_gym.human.DEFAULT_KEYS, rushhour-gym>=0.7.0): S/D and 3/4 choose, the arrows slide by direction, 1/2 (or , .) slide back/forward. Choosing calls no engine step; a slide becomes the engine's Discrete(32) action, logged as env_action (-1 for a choose or a wrong-axis press). Keep the message Controls text in sync with 'keys' by hand.",
  "_keys_reference": "https://github.com/chrplr/Rush-Hour/blob/main/python/src/rushhour_gym/human.py",
  "_ergonomics_note": "White triangles on the selected car show which of its two slides are legal (from the engine action_mask), because a refused move renders identically to a dropped keypress. 'movable_only' (default true, matching Rush-Hour's -movable-only default) makes selection skip cars that cannot move at all. false is Rush-Hour's -movable-only=false: a different task, since noticing which cars are stuck becomes part of the search -- keep it the same across sessions being compared.",
  "curriculum": [
    {
      "type": "message",
      "align": "left",
      "text": [
        "RUSH HOUR",
        "",
        "On each puzzle, get the RED car out through the opening",
        "on the right wall.",
        "",
        "Vehicles only slide along their own axis, and cannot pass",
        "through each other.",
        "",
        "One car is outlined in white; the arrows on it show which ways",
        "it can still move.",
        "",
        "Cars that cannot move at all are skipped: choosing only ever",
        "lands on a car with at least one arrow.",
        "",
        "   S / D  or  3 / 4          choose the previous / next car",
        "   LEFT / RIGHT              slide a horizontal car left / right",
        "   UP / DOWN                 slide a vertical car up / down",
        "   1 or ,   /   2 or .       slide it left or up / right or down",
        "",
        "There are 49 puzzles. The first 12 get harder one by one;",
        "after that, easy and hard ones come in any order.",
        "Take all the time you need.",
        "",
        "(press SPACE to begin)"
      ]
    },
    {"type": "fixation", "duration": 1.0},
